wishnode.net — Minutes Publisher page content phext (phexts/minutes-publisher.phext). Source of truth for the Minutes Publisher page. Task 34.6.6/7.7.10/7.6.10. See docs/COORDINATE-MAP.md.Minutes Publisher — Exocortexia | Phext, Inc.Minutes PublisherOpen Meetings Act compliance publishing · Neb. Rev. Stat. §§ 84-1408–84-1414SUMMARY (plain English — rendered first)Minutes Publisher helps boards post meeting agendas and minutes on time. It checks each meeting against Nebraska's Open Meetings Act and flags late posts at once. It is for clerks and staff of boards, counties, cities, and schools.What it doesPublic bodies must post an agenda before each meeting and minutes after it. The law sets hard due dates for both. This tool tracks each meeting and checks the dates for you.The demo tracks twelve made-up boards — example boards, not real counties — with six meetings each. Each agenda and each set of minutes is checked the day it is posted.Late is shown as late. If a post is still inside its time window, the tool says it is pending, not late. That is fair, and it is what the law says.Proof of postingEach post is written into a tamper-proof record — every entry is locked to the one before it, so if anyone changes a past entry, the change shows. So “we posted it on time” is a fact you can check, not a claim you must take on faith.The tool can print one clean page with all boards and meetings. The page ends with the log entries and the result of the check on the log. A board can post that page as its public proof.What the law saysAgenda: the agenda must be ready for the public at least 24 hours before the meeting.Minutes: minutes must be written and ready to view in 10 working days, or before the next meeting if that comes first.Keeping minutes: some bodies must keep minutes for at least 6 months. That rule covers natural resources districts, cities, school boards, and counties with more than 25,000 people. Smaller counties are not named in that rule. They still follow their own records schedules.The Act does not define “working days.” This tool counts Monday to Friday as working days. That is our reading of the law, and we state it in the open so you can judge it.Full statute sheet: see the Minutes Publisher part of the statutes phext on this site. The page link reads “How Minutes Publisher matches Nebraska law.”Good to knowAll twelve boards in the demo are made up. Each name says “Example.” Board members and votes are made up too.The demo checks the 24-hour agenda rule by day, not by the hour. A live setup would use exact times.In the test run, the log held 72 agenda posts and 65 minutes posts. Seven sets of minutes were left unposted on purpose, so the checks had late cases to catch.The suite is checked by 84 automated tests. Seven of them check this program.Ask about a pilotAsk about a pilot for your office. Use the form on this site — the link says “Ask about a pilot.” Tell us which program you are interested in and what your office needs. When you send the form, you get a tracking code. Keep it. You can use it on this site to check the state of your request at any time. We read every request, and a person from Phext, Inc. replies. We will talk through what a small pilot would look like for your office. There is no cost to ask, and asking does not sign you up for anything.Links on this page (descriptive labels)Read the Minutes Publisher source code (for programmers) — Source tree in the Exocortexia repositoryHow Minutes Publisher matches Nebraska law — Statute sheet, also held in phexts/statutes.phextFOR TECHNICAL STAFF — not main copy. Component: apps/minutes-publisher · Default port: 8802 · Tests: 7 in this app, 69 workspace-wide · Working-day math: weekday = (day + 4) mod 7 with day 0 = 1970-01-01 (Thursday); working days Mon–Fri; window counted in (meeting_day, posted_day] · Sign-in: edit.phext.io SSO when configured; open demo mode otherwise · Source: github.com/wbic16/Exocortexia/tree/exo/apps/minutes-publisher · Statute sheet source: docs/minutes-publisher-statute.md.Original page text before the plain-English rewrite (provenance):
